WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:06.020
The Marvel comics canon is filled with thousands
of characters, and the MCU has done a darn

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:09.240
good job bringing many of them to the silver
screen.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:13.920
Still, there are a few heroes and villains
who don't seem to be joining the franchise

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:14.920
anytime soon.

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:19.560
Given her massive spike in popularity over
the last decade, it seems pretty odd that

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:23.270
Squirrel Girl has yet to make an appearance
in the MCU.

00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:27.970
For a while, it was expected that she'd make
her live-action debut in a New Warriors TV

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:32.870
series, but that project fell apart shortly
before that version of Marvel Television ceased

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:33.969
to exist.

00:00:33.969 --> 00:00:35.760
So who is Squirrel Girl?

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:41.499
The character of Doreen Green first appeared
in 1991, but only became a certifiably marquee

00:00:41.499 --> 00:00:46.980
superhero when Ryan North and Erica Henderson's
The Unbeatable Squirrel Girl became a surprise

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:49.079
hit in 2015.

00:00:49.079 --> 00:00:54.089
Unbeatable merges a kid-friendly, positive
outlook on the world with the kind of tongue-in-cheek

00:00:54.089 --> 00:00:58.609
self-awareness that Howard The Duck did so
well in the 1970s.

00:00:58.609 --> 00:01:03.489
Sadly, it seems like Kevin Feige and company
haven't found quite the right moment to merge

00:01:03.489 --> 00:01:09.170
the unique sensibility of Unbeatable Squirrel
Girl with the MCU's far less quirky approach.

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:13.950
Still, Squirrel Girl does at least make regular
appearances in the Marvel Rising animated

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:17.710
series, so the character doesn't seem to have
been totally abandoned.

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:24.710
"Guy covered in squirrels says 'what'?"

00:01:24.710 --> 00:01:27.659
"What?"

00:01:27.659 --> 00:01:28.710
Maybe there's hope yet.

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:34.740
The 2015 co-licensing arrangement between
Sony and Marvel Studios yielded Peter Parker's

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:40.240
MCU premiere in Captain America: Civil War,
a number of standalone follow-ups, and a leading

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:42.859
role in the more recent Avengers movies.

00:01:42.859 --> 00:01:45.220
But here's a question: what about Venom?

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:50.720
This 2018 Spidey spin-off appeared to be a
strictly-Sony project at the time, and it

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:56.229
was understood that the studio was forging
its own separate Marvel universe using Spider-Man's

00:01:56.229 --> 00:01:57.409
rogues' gallery.

00:01:57.409 --> 00:02:00.560
"Well, you know, I aim to please."

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:02.590
That seems to have changed, though.

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:07.649
After all, if the upcoming Morbius isn't supposed
to be attached to the MCU, then what the heck

00:02:07.649 --> 00:02:11.410
is Michael Keaton's Adrian Toomes doing in
the movie's trailer?

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:17.070
And if the MCU's Vulture is connected to Morbius,
and Morbius is connected to Venom, does that

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:21.130
retroactively make Tom Hardy the MCU's Venom
after all?

00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:23.580
So yeah, things are a little confusing right
now.

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:28.120
Sony has more spin-off movies coming up too,
including a project helmed by Olivia Wilde,

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:31.850
a movie based on Madame Web, and a Kraven
the Hunter flick.

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:35.780
For now, it seems like those characters — as
well as Venom and Morbius — aren't likely

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:40.050
to appear in the MCU anytime soon — keyword
“soon”.

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:44.319
But if Vulture's surprise appearance in the
Sony Universe is anything to go by, well,

00:02:44.319 --> 00:02:46.140
let's just say "never say never."

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:52.100
First appearing in 2000's Marvel Boy #1, Noh-Varr
is already pretty well-suited to front his

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:53.100
own movie.

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:58.530
He's got a vast range of crazy powers, including
mind-control saliva, explosive fingernail

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:04.670
clippings, superhuman strength and lightning-fast
reflexes, as well as all sorts of sci-fi weapons

00:03:04.670 --> 00:03:09.700
to hand — which he can use to laser the
face off of virtually any cosmic or terrestrial

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:10.700
threat.

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:15.640
He's also a Kree from another dimension, so
integrating him into the MCU wouldn't be particularly

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:16.640
difficult.

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:20.540
The Kree first showed up in Guardians of the
Galaxy and were expanded upon by Captain Marvel

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:21.910
a few years later.

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:27.720
So sure, Marvel Boy may not be leading the
charge into the MCU's cosmic realm just yet,

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:31.930
but whenever Feige decides he wants to bring
in another arrogant weirdo from outer space

00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:35.410
to kick some alien butt — well, Noh-Varr
will be ready.

00:03:36.410 --> 00:03:41.020
The rumors regarding Heather Douglas' future
cinematic status tend to be a little inconsistent.

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:45.880
In the past, the star-hopping telepath's sexuality
has often been cited as being too much for

00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:48.230
Disney's often conservative sensibilities.

00:03:48.230 --> 00:03:53.680
Now, though, there are at least a few rumblings
that Drax the Destroyer's in-comics daughter

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:58.590
might become his onscreen daughter, and that
it could happen as soon as the third Guardians

00:03:58.590 --> 00:03:59.590
movie.

00:03:59.590 --> 00:04:03.150
Still, Marvel Studios hasn't revealed any
clear cut plans to bring the character into

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:05.650
the fold, so don't go holding your breath.

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:07.600
There's also something else to consider, here.

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:12.440
The comic book version of Mantis, who is originally
portrayed as a human martial arts master,

00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:16.090
is basically nothing like the character who
showed up in the MCU.

00:04:16.090 --> 00:04:20.660
But Moondragon in the comics is a telepath
who wears green and spends most of her time

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:22.030
in outer space.

00:04:22.030 --> 00:04:28.060
And that, in a nutshell, is exactly why Moondragon
may never properly appear in the MCU — because,

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:29.770
in a sense, she's already there.

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:34.690
It actually feels like a bit of a disservice
to lump together Alpha Flight in one big group.

00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:40.291
In particular, Puck and Sasquatch have arguably
had as much of an impact on the Marvel Comics

00:04:40.291 --> 00:04:45.160
continuity as some of the publisher's more
currently prominent characters.

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:50.060
It's also worth noting that there's no indigenous
representation in the MCU lineup right now,

00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:53.310
which is an issue many Alpha Flight characters
could help resolve.

00:04:53.310 --> 00:05:01.290
Debuting in 1979's Uncanny X-Men #120, Alpha
Flight plays a significant part in the pre-X-Men

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:06.180
phase of Wolverine's superhero career, and
the squad often includes mutant siblings Aurora

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:07.410
and Northstar.

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:11.290
Considering their long history with the world
of mutants, it seems strange that Fox never

00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:13.810
took the opportunity to bring them to the
silver screen.

00:05:13.810 --> 00:05:19.030
Sadly, it doesn't seem like Marvel Studios
are keen to do so, either, as no Alpha Flight-related

00:05:19.030 --> 00:05:22.960
projects have currently been announced, and
there doesn't seem to be any space for them

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:26.640
to show up in the studio's upcoming projects
— for now, at least.

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:32.340
If Rick Jones had never wandered around Bruce
Banner's bomb testing site like the oblivious

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:36.730
ditz that he is, then Bruce Banner would never
have been exposed to the gamma rays that turn

00:05:36.730 --> 00:05:38.060
him into the Hulk.

00:05:38.060 --> 00:05:42.270
Putting aside his connections to Captain America
and Mar-Vell, that fact alone makes Jones

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:45.310
an under-used part of Marvel Comics continuity.

00:05:45.310 --> 00:05:50.000
In a sense, he does actually exist in the
MCU too, since his name briefly appears on

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:55.230
a computer screen in the first Avengers movie
— so he's clearly off doing his thing somewhere

00:05:55.230 --> 00:05:56.830
in Marvel's movie world.

00:05:56.830 --> 00:05:59.530
But he still hasn't made an actual appearance.

00:05:59.530 --> 00:06:06.240
Between Jones' failure to appear in the MCU,
Jason Todd's macabre DCEU legacy, and Jimmy

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:12.370
Olsen's unceremonious death in Batman v Superman,
it feels like the movie studios really don't

00:06:12.370 --> 00:06:17.400
like the idea of bringing any youthful, ordinary
sidekicks into their cinematic universes.

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:22.620
Nonetheless, the storylines with which Jones
is most often associated are still ongoing,

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:27.270
including the Kree-Skrull War and Bruce Banner's
many scientific experiments — so there are

00:06:27.270 --> 00:06:31.580
at least a few opportunities left to bring
this vital yet lesser-known character into

00:06:31.580 --> 00:06:32.580
the MCU.

00:06:33.580 --> 00:06:38.060
In the current run of comics, Hulkling is
more widely recognized as Dorrek VIII, Emperor

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:40.120
of the Kree-Skrull Alliance.

00:06:40.120 --> 00:06:44.170
And if that's basically nonsense to you, think
about how powerful and important the American

00:06:44.170 --> 00:06:49.490
president is, multiply that times outer space,
and throw a sun-destroying superweapon into

00:06:49.490 --> 00:06:54.860
the mix — and you should get a rough idea
what the son of Mar-Vell has on his plate.

00:06:54.860 --> 00:07:01.449
He's also noteworthy as half of one of Marvel's
most prominent LGBTQ couples, alongside his

00:07:01.449 --> 00:07:03.680
Young Avengers teammate Wiccan.

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:07.650
Although the MCU's version of the Skrulls
only popped up for the first time in Captain

00:07:07.650 --> 00:07:12.490
Marvel, they're due to be expanded on with
the upcoming Secret Invasion show and, most

00:07:12.490 --> 00:07:14.270
likely, Captain Marvel 2.

00:07:14.270 --> 00:07:18.680
And then you've the other members of Hulkling's
generation of Young Avengers, many of whom

00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:22.510
are set to gradually show up in the MCU in
the next few years.

00:07:22.510 --> 00:07:27.580
These include Kate Bishop, who will make her
debut in the Disney+ Hawkeye series, America

00:07:27.580 --> 00:07:32.860
Chavez, who is set to appear in Doctor Strange
in the Multiverse of Madness, and both Speed

00:07:32.860 --> 00:07:36.760
and Wiccan, who have already technically appeared
in WandaVision.

00:07:36.760 --> 00:07:42.030
And that's on top of Cassie Lang, who has
featured in a number of MCU movies.

00:07:42.030 --> 00:07:46.430
Nonetheless, although the pieces are certainly
in place for Hulkling to make his big screen

00:07:46.430 --> 00:07:50.760
debut sometime in the near future, nothing
solid has yet been announced.

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:52.820
Here's hoping it's not too much of a wait.

00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:57.830
One strange aspect of the comic book industry
is the fact that, over the last 60 years,

00:07:57.830 --> 00:08:02.010
just a small handful of close-knit writers
and artists were responsible for creating

00:08:02.010 --> 00:08:05.580
the overwhelming majority of superheroes and
their stories.

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:09.960
And this is pretty much exactly the reason
that Man-Thing can be found mucking about

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:14.570
in the swamplands of the Marvel Universe,
while Swamp Thing lurks in the boggy marshes

00:08:14.570 --> 00:08:16.550
of the DC universe.

00:08:16.550 --> 00:08:22.389
Man-Thing actually had a movie, in 2005, but
still, despite debuting prior to DC’s muck

00:08:22.389 --> 00:08:27.600
monster, Swamp Thing is much better known
- thanks in large part to the 2019 Swamp Thing

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:32.430
TV series and the cult classic 1982 film of
the same name.

00:08:32.430 --> 00:08:39.639
"Everything's a dream… when you're alone."

00:08:39.639 --> 00:08:44.879
But that hasn't quite kept the creature formerly
known as Dr. Ted Sallis out of the MCU entirely.

00:08:44.879 --> 00:08:50.490
His likeness appears in Thor: Ragnarok, alongside
tributes to Beta Ray Bill, Ares, and Bi-Beast

00:08:50.490 --> 00:08:53.230
on the Grandmaster's Palace on Sakaar.

00:08:53.230 --> 00:08:55.089
This raises a whole new problem, though.

00:08:55.089 --> 00:09:01.350
If Man-Thing, Beta Ray Bill, Ares, and Bi-Beast
are all celebrated as past champions of Sakaar's

00:09:01.350 --> 00:09:05.820
contest, doesn't that mean that they each
would have been defeated by the champion who

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:07.240
came after them?

00:09:07.240 --> 00:09:11.670
Basically: is the MCU's Man-Thing already
dead… and could Hulk have been the one to

00:09:11.670 --> 00:09:12.670
kill him?

00:09:13.670 --> 00:09:17.040
Discussions about superhero comics in the
'80s and '90s tend to focus on the moodier

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:20.920
aspects of dark, moody releases like Alan
Moore's Watchmen.

00:09:20.920 --> 00:09:25.629
But people tend to overlook the snarkier,
more self-aware side of those very same stories.

00:09:25.629 --> 00:09:29.740
And while characters like Rorschach and Dr.
Manhattan have certainly made a bigger splash

00:09:29.740 --> 00:09:34.279
in the pop culture consciousness than Nite-Owl,
that doesn't mean he didn't have a legacy

00:09:34.279 --> 00:09:35.470
of his own.

00:09:35.470 --> 00:09:41.439
First introduced in 1988 by Tom DeFalco and
Spider-Man co-creator Steve Ditko, Robbie

00:09:41.439 --> 00:09:46.980
Baldwin has powers related to kinetic energy
that allow him to, well.... bounce off stuff.

00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:51.170
He can also cause the sudden appearance of
masses of multicolored balls.

00:09:51.170 --> 00:09:52.730
Because why not?

00:09:52.730 --> 00:09:57.040
Following the unfortunate events that led
to the original Civil War storyline, Baldwin

00:09:57.040 --> 00:10:01.610
changed his alias and costume to the grim
Penance, then later smartened up and went

00:10:01.610 --> 00:10:03.139
back to being Speedball.

00:10:03.139 --> 00:10:07.189
Like Squirrel Girl, Speedball would actually
have made his debut in the New Warriors TV

00:10:07.189 --> 00:10:12.000
series, but with that show long since axed,
it doesn't look like Robbie will be bouncing

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:15.129
his way into the MCU anytime soon.

00:10:16.129 --> 00:10:19.730
The Shi'ar are a universe-spanning society
of technologically advanced humanoids who

00:10:19.730 --> 00:10:24.240
usually find themselves tangled up in the
X-Men's mythology, and are perhaps most famous

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:26.670
for their role in the Dark Phoenix saga.

00:10:26.670 --> 00:10:31.019
For some reason, though, Fox elected to use
a lesser-known alien race to fill the Shi'ar's

00:10:31.019 --> 00:10:33.389
role in 2019's X-Men: Dark Phoenix.

00:10:33.389 --> 00:10:38.179
Did that decision have anything to do with
the ambiguous nature of the Shi'ar's licensing

00:10:38.179 --> 00:10:39.179
rights?

00:10:39.179 --> 00:10:43.170
Well, no one really knows for sure — but
it's definitely a possibility, and might explain

00:10:43.170 --> 00:10:48.149
why Marvel has been equally hesitant to involve
the Shi'ar in their cinematic universe.

00:10:48.149 --> 00:10:52.269
It doesn't help that, once you remove the
context of Dark Phoenix, the Shi'ar basically

00:10:52.269 --> 00:10:56.389
come off as Kryptonians with a home planet
that hasn't exploded.

00:10:56.389 --> 00:10:59.019
They don't exactly win a ton of points for
originality.

00:10:59.019 --> 00:11:03.630
Still, the MCU has taken plenty of influence
from the cosmic corner of Marvel comics, so

00:11:03.630 --> 00:11:07.720
fans should have every reason to believe the
Shi'ar might make their presence felt in the

00:11:07.720 --> 00:11:11.399
years to come — especially once the X-Men
get rolling again.

00:11:12.399 --> 00:11:16.410
He's the run-down on Darkhawk: he wears a
suit of armor that fires off blasts of energy,

00:11:16.410 --> 00:11:17.769
just like Iron Man.

00:11:17.769 --> 00:11:22.389
That suit includes a pair of retractable claws,
which are very similar to Wolverine's.

00:11:22.389 --> 00:11:26.800
It's also generated by an amulet that Christopher
Powell discovered while being chased by mobsters,

00:11:26.800 --> 00:11:31.399
much in the same way that Danny Ketch stumbles
upon the enchanted motorcycle that turns him

00:11:31.399 --> 00:11:32.490
into Ghost Rider.

00:11:32.490 --> 00:11:36.410
Honestly, it's a wonder that Marvel never
sued themselves for copyright infringement

00:11:36.410 --> 00:11:41.470
— and even if they didn't, they're probably
not itching to stick him in a movie anytime

00:11:41.470 --> 00:11:42.470
soon.

00:11:42.470 --> 00:11:45.910
Then again, Darkhawk's costume was one of
the more memorable Marvel designs to emerge

00:11:45.910 --> 00:11:50.989
from the zany early '90s, and there is some
merit to the notion of bringing the character

00:11:50.989 --> 00:11:56.220
to the silver screen, so hey — with a few
fresh abilities and a brand new origin story,

00:11:56.220 --> 00:11:57.569
it just might work.

00:11:57.569 --> 00:11:59.329
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:11:59.329 --> 00:12:03.249
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
comic characters are coming soon.

00:12:03.249 --> 00:12:06.290
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit the
bell so you don't miss a single one.

